1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,840
Kevin runs a mobile dog grooming business.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:07,120
This MVP brings his booking requests into one clear workflow.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,000
His problem is simple: requests get lost in text messages.

4
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,600
The app gives him one place to review and manage them.

5
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:18,570
Customers sign in to their own account, then request a visit with their dog’s

6
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:21,320
details, the service and a preferred date.

7
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,260
They can also add an optional dog photo and preview it before sending.

8
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:30,740
The saved image is private to the customer and administrator.

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:36,680
A preferred date is a request, not a guaranteed appointment.

10
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,860
Kevin still agrees on the exact time and price with the customer.

11
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:45,290
Contact information, the service address and care notes are collected

12
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:50,160
together. For this walkthrough, all the booking details are fictional.

13
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:53,060
The customer gets a receipt and a reference number.

14
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:57,920
The screen clearly explains that the appointment is not confirmed yet.

15
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:01,500
Kevin receives the new request in his workspace.

16
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:06,680
The unread indicator and request list make it easier to see who needs a reply.

17
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:11,790
Opening a request brings together the dog, service, customer details and care

18
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,820
notes. Kevin can contact the customer before agreeing on the visit.

19
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:21,940
To confirm, Kevin enters the start time, appointment duration and travel time.

20
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:27,110
Here, a visit starts at ten, lasts one hour and reserves twenty minutes for

21
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:28,160
travel.

22
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:31,760
That reserves the schedule from ten until eleven twenty.

23
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:36,290
The database blocks any confirmed appointment that overlaps the visit or its

24
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:37,340
travel buffer.

25
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,360
An attempt to confirm another visit at ten thirty is rejected.

26
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:44,420
The request stays pending, so Kevin can choose a free time.

27
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:48,100
At eleven twenty, the next visit can be confirmed.

28
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,050
The previous appointment and its travel buffer have finished, so there is no

29
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:52,450
overlap.

30
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:58,010
For real bookings, Brevo sends confirmation and cancellation emails to the

31
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:04,420
customer’s verified account. This isolated practice workspace sends no email.

32
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:08,300
If a confirmed visit is cancelled, its time is released.

33
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:12,810
The request remains in the system, and a real customer receives a cancellation

34
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:13,860
email.

35
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:18,160
Permanent deletion is a separate action with an explicit warning.

36
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:21,990
Here I keep the request; I do not delete it.

37
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:25,160
Sites and Cloudflare host the application.

38
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:30,330
Supabase manages accounts and data, Brevo sends booking emails, and the source

39
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,100
code is available on GitHub.

40
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:36,480
This MVP assumes one groomer.

41
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:41,260
Route calculation and closed-browser push alerts are future improvements.

42
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:44,940
I used AI to help build and test the solution.
